0:00:00.000,0:00:04.240
supporting algorithm reasoning 
through executable sketching

0:00:04.240,0:00:06.640
When students learn a new algorithm,

0:00:06.640,0:00:11.360
they are often taught through step-by-step 
descriptions and visual animations.

0:00:11.360,0:00:16.080
An algorithm is essentially a recipe, 
a sequence of steps to follow.

0:00:16.080,0:00:20.560
However, understanding an algorithm 
is not just about reading or watching.

0:00:20.560,0:00:24.640
Students need to mentally walk through 
the steps to truly digest them.

0:00:24.640,0:00:29.360
At this stage, they may not yet know 
how to implement the algorithm in code

0:00:29.360,0:00:34.000
and are just beginning to build a 
mental model of how the algorithm works.

0:00:34.000,0:00:37.440
So how can they tell whether 
they truly understand it?

0:00:37.440,0:00:43.760
One common way to check their understanding is to 
walk through the algorithm in a concrete example,

0:00:43.760,0:00:46.720
sketching each step along the way.

0:00:46.720,0:00:52.160
Let's say Alice starts with 
this array, 5, 2, 3, 4, 1.

0:00:52.160,0:00:54.080
She begins step by step.

0:00:54.080,0:01:00.640
She compares 5 and 2, swaps them, and 
compares 5 and 3, and then 5 and 4.

0:01:00.640,0:01:06.800
As she continues, the trace quickly grows longer.
The layout becomes much less organized.

0:01:06.800,0:01:10.960
She moves to a new page and 
copies the current state.

0:01:10.960,0:01:13.520
But here, she missed a swap.

0:01:13.520,0:01:16.640
She continues without even noticing this mistake.

0:01:16.640,0:01:22.400
To make it faster, she also starts skipping 
some steps, doing parts of it mentally.

0:01:22.400,0:01:27.280
When Alice looks back at her note, it's 
difficult to reconstruct the full process.

0:01:27.280,0:01:29.680
The sketches don't have a clear structure.

0:01:29.680,0:01:33.120
It's also hard to reuse this 
process for other inputs.

0:01:33.120,0:01:37.840
What she has is just a messy 
record for one specific example.

0:01:37.840,0:01:42.480
To address these challenges, we 
present SketchMind to help users

0:01:42.480,0:01:47.040
externalize, execute, and check their 
understanding of a given algorithm.

0:01:47.040,0:01:50.320
First, SketchMind supports executable tracing.

0:01:50.320,0:01:55.360
Users can use different pen tools 
to execute operations in real time.

0:01:55.360,0:02:01.200
In BubbleSort, we provide a toolbox of 
primitives, such as compare and switch.

0:02:01.200,0:02:07.680
Alice sketches an array on the left canvas, 
and the system recognizes it as an array.

0:02:07.680,0:02:11.200
She selects the compare 
tool and marks two numbers.

0:02:11.200,0:02:16.880
The result appears on the right-hand 
panel, showing that 3 is greater than 1.

0:02:16.880,0:02:20.240
She then uses the swap tool to exchange them.

0:02:20.240,0:02:24.800
SketchMind supports diverse drawing 
styles, allowing users to sketch

0:02:24.800,0:02:31.120
naturally while maintaining a clear, 
replayable record of their reasoning.

0:02:31.120,0:02:36.640
Building on this, SketchMind allows users 
to merge steps into high-level logic,

0:02:36.640,0:02:42.160
such as if-else conditions or loops, 
which can then be reused as new tools.

0:02:42.800,0:02:47.040
Alice noticed that she often compares 
two elements and then swaps them,

0:02:47.040,0:02:53.200
so she combines these into a single conditional 
operation and creates a reusable pen.

0:02:53.200,0:02:57.040
She can now use this pen 
to perform the next round.

0:02:57.040,0:03:01.760
To support progressive abstraction, 
SketchMind provides three views:

0:03:01.760,0:03:05.440
concrete values, index, and parameters.

0:03:05.440,0:03:10.640
Alice noticed that things look very 
similar from one step to the next.

0:03:10.640,0:03:14.720
She confirms this intuition by 
switching to the parameter view,

0:03:14.720,0:03:20.960
where she thinks the same operation is 
repeated three times for different values of i.

0:03:20.960,0:03:23.840
So she groups these steps together into a loop,

0:03:23.840,0:03:27.520
showing how the element 
bubbles up during each pass.

0:03:27.520,0:03:33.520
When a user submits an incomplete solution, 
instead of directly giving feedback,

0:03:33.520,0:03:38.880
SketchMind generates counterexamples so 
the learner can see their own mistake.

0:03:38.880,0:03:46.320
When Alice clicks Done, the system generates 
a counterexample by changing 2 to 0.

0:03:46.320,0:03:50.640
Replaying her sketches shows 
that the array is not sorted.

0:03:50.640,0:03:54.080
Alice finds that her plan 
only works on one example.

0:03:54.080,0:03:57.120
She is missing the last phase of the algorithm.

0:03:57.120,0:03:59.920
She then returns to the canvas.

0:03:59.920,0:04:06.880
Alice realizes that to sort an array of 4 
numbers, BubbleSort requires 3 full passes.

0:04:06.880,0:04:11.120
Now, when she clicks down, 
all examples are correct.

0:04:11.120,0:04:15.760
To show that the previous example 
does not complete all required passes,

0:04:15.760,0:04:19.200
we generate a counterexample with two properties.

0:04:19.200,0:04:23.600
First, all of the student's 
earlier steps remain valid.

0:04:23.600,0:04:28.080
Second, the new example requires 
additional steps to finish.

0:04:28.080,0:04:33.040
This helps reduce student confusion 
while still exposing the mistake.

0:04:33.040,0:04:38.000
In the paper, we use a logic solver 
to construct this counterexample.

0:04:38.000,0:04:42.400
More generally, SketchMind can 
be extended to other algorithms

0:04:42.400,0:04:45.360
by defining customized primitives.

0:04:45.360,0:04:50.240
We explore pancake sort by repeatedly 
finding the maximum element, and then

0:04:50.240,0:04:54.320
flipping twice to move it to its final position.

0:04:54.320,0:04:58.400
For more details about sketchMind, 
please refer to our paper.
